          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "EXAMPLE 1: FLICKER\n\nIn this example we wanted to create a flickering effect.\nBy using unipolar noise to modulate the gain on the fader we create rapid changes in the opacity of the image, resulting in the flickering effect."
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 455.5625,
          "width": 956.9999389648436,
          "x": -33.90003204345703,
          "y": 59.28433990478515
        },
        "class": {
          "id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
          "version": 1
        },
        "clock": "video",
        "color": "ffff6a00",
        "constants": {},
        "hidden": [
          "text",
          "alignment",
          "font-size",
          "fill"
        ],
        "name": "Group",
        "thumbnail_visible": true
      },
      "67": {
        "attributes": {
          "alignment": {
            "type": "integer",
            "value": 0
          },
          "fill": {
            "type": "bool",
            "value": false
          },
          "font-size": {
            "type": "float",
            "value": 19
          },
          "text": {
            "type": "string",
            "value": "EXAMPLE 2: SMOOTH NOISE\n\nSmoothing the noise is a nice way to create less chaotic effects.\nIn this example we modulate the amount of smoothing with a Sine oscillator.\nThis allows us to transition between the full randomness of the noise and a smoothed version of the same signal.\n\nNote that if you want continuously smoothed noise you might be better off using the Perlin Generator node.\n"
          }
        },
        "bounds": {
          "height": 544.8246459960938,
          "width": 1211.9998779296875,
          "x": -35.376609802246094,
          "y": 559.5484008789062
